/** @page pythonreleasenotesguide python Module Release Notes

    @tableofcontents

    @section python_1_3 python Module Version 1.3
    <b>Security:</b>
    - %Python code can no longer be run from %Qore Programs restricted with
      @ref Qore::PO_NO_UNCONTROLLED_APIS "PO_NO_UNCONTROLLED_APIS" (included in
      @ref Qore::PO_NO_EXTERNAL_ACCESS "PO_NO_EXTERNAL_ACCESS", @ref Qore::PO_NO_IO "PO_NO_IO" and
      @ref Qore::PO_LOCKDOWN "PO_LOCKDOWN"): the @ref Python::PythonProgram "PythonProgram" class is now in the
      \c UNCONTROLLED_API functional domain as well as in \c EMBEDDED_LOGIC, and the \c python module commands are
      rejected in such Programs (and \c parse also with @ref Qore::PO_NO_EMBEDDED_LOGIC "PO_NO_EMBEDDED_LOGIC"), also
      when the %Program inherits the module from its parent.  Previously such Programs could run any %Python code,
      which is not subject to %Qore's restrictions (<a href="https://github.com/qoretechnologies/qore/issues/5506">issue
      5506</a>); see @ref python_restrictions

    <b>New Features and Enhancements:</b>
    - Added support for Python 3.12, 3.13, 3.14, 3.15, and 3.16 (tested with the first alpha release of 3.16)
    - Added support for the free-threading mode (PEP 703) of Python 3.14 and later
    - The minimum supported %Python version is now 3.11
    - @ref Qore::SandboxManager::requestInterrupt() "SandboxManager::requestInterrupt()" interrupts the %Python code
      of sandboxed programs: it is checked before acquiring the Python GIL
      (<a href="https://github.com/qoretechnologies/qore/issues/5116">issue 5116</a>), and %Python code that runs is
      interrupted with \c PROGRAM-INTERRUPTED (see @ref python_interrupts;
      <a href="https://github.com/qoretechnologies/qore/issues/5502">issue 5502</a>)
    - Added the @ref Python::PythonVersion "PythonVersion" constant providing the Python version string
    - Added @ref Python::PythonProgram::getAttribute() "PythonProgram::getAttribute()" and
      @ref Python::PythonProgram::setAttribute() "PythonProgram::setAttribute()" to read and set the global variables
      of a %Python program (<a href="https://github.com/qoretechnologies/qore/issues/5501">issue 5501</a>)
    - %Qore code can call %Python functions and methods with keyword arguments with the new \c keyword_arguments
      argument of @ref Python::PythonProgram::callFunctionArgs() "PythonProgram::callFunctionArgs()" and
      @ref Python::PythonProgram::callMethodArgs() "PythonProgram::callMethodArgs()" (see
      @ref python_keyword_arguments; <a href="https://github.com/qoretechnologies/qore/issues/5498">issue 5498</a>)

    - Calls between %Qore and %Python have less overhead: a call from %Qore to a %Python function acquires the
      %Python context once (it was acquired twice), calls from %Python to %Qore do not track the argument tuple for
      cycle detection, and releasing %Python references with the GIL held does not acquire the GIL state again
      (\c PythonProgram::callFunction() about 18% faster, calls from %Python to %Qore methods and call references
      about 12-15% faster; see \c test/benchmark-calls.qr;
      <a href="https://github.com/qoretechnologies/qore/issues/5503">issue 5503</a>)

    <b>Bug Fixes:</b>
    - automatically imported %Python classes and their bases use the namespace of their defining module
      (\c __module__), independent of import order: importing \c os creates
      \c Python::collections::abc::Iterable, rather than \c Python::os::Iterable. This also changes the paths
      of classes re-exported by other modules; use their defining-module paths. Function and constant imports
      and explicit \c export-class paths are unchanged. Errors reading class namespace metadata are propagated
      safely (<a href="https://github.com/qoretechnologies/qore/issues/5520">issue 5520</a>)
    - values nested too deeply for the stack (in either direction) raise an exception instead of crashing; values
      that %Python code changes while they are converted (ex: in \c tzinfo.utcoffset()) are converted safely;
      \c str subclass keys (ex: \c StrEnum members) are strings; \c datetime.time subclasses are converted;
      invalid UTC offsets raise the %Python exception; \c __all__ entries and module names with lone surrogates no
      longer crash; the %Python object of a %Qore object is not reused while it is deallocated (weak reference
      callbacks), and calling \c __init__() again raises \c RuntimeError; a %Qore class inheriting a %Python subclass
      of a %Qore class uses its own %Qore object; interrupting a %Python thread that calls %Qore no longer deadlocks;
      with %Python 3.12, %Qore exceptions in threads created by %Python no longer crash; %Qore objects held by the
      %Python objects of a %Program are released while the %Program is valid (their destructors raised
      \c PROGRAM-ERROR) (<a href="https://github.com/qoretechnologies/qore/issues/5515">issue 5515</a>)
    - the %Python types of %Qore classes, hashdecls and enums no longer crash when they are used after the
      %Program that declared them has been deleted (ex: creating an object or a hash of a type kept in a %Python
      variable, calling a method or reading a static member): they raise \c RuntimeError; the declarations of a new
      %Program get new types; a hash of a hashdecl that outlived its %Program (held by a %Qore value) is a \c dict,
      the value of such an enum is the value of its member, and an object of such a class raises
      \c PYTHON-VALUE-ERROR (requires %Qore 3.0;
      <a href="https://github.com/qoretechnologies/qore/issues/5517">issue 5517</a>)
    - documentation: \c PythonProgram::evalStatement() and \c PythonProgram::evalStatementKeep() document where they
      run (\c evalStatement() in the \c __main__ module of the interpreter, \c evalStatementKeep() in the globals of
      the program) and that they return no value, \c PythonProgram::run() that it runs the code again, the static
      methods that they use the %Python program of the current %Program, the type guide \c None, objects, enums and
      hashdecls; the \c export-class and \c export-func examples work, and the errors of the \c parse and \c alias
      module commands give their syntax (\c parse gave the syntax of \c alias)
      (<a href="https://github.com/qoretechnologies/qore/issues/5505">issue 5505</a>)
    - \c qoreloader.issue_module_cmd() and \c qoreloader.load_java() raise \c UnicodeEncodeError for strings that
      cannot be encoded in UTF-8 (ex: a lone surrogate); they crashed; errors initializing the %Python program of a
      %Qore %Program (ex: when \c qoreloader cannot be imported) are raised in the %Program that loads the module,
      which then cannot use %Python, and \c import \c qoreloader raises \c ImportError if the %Qore \c python
      module cannot be loaded or the thread cannot be registered with %Qore (the process was ended with \c exit(1),
      or the import failed without an exception) (<a href="https://github.com/qoretechnologies/qore/issues/5504">issue
      5504</a>)
    - \c import \c qoreloader no longer blocks the signals of the %Python process and of the processes that it
      starts (ex: \c Popen.terminate() was ignored and \c SIGINT never arrived): %Qore is initialized without its
      signal handling when %Python is the host; deleting a @ref Python::PythonProgram "PythonProgram" while another
      thread runs its %Python code interrupts the code instead of waiting forever
      (<a href="https://github.com/qoretechnologies/qore/issues/5502">issue 5502</a>)
    - @ref Python::PythonProgram::callFunction() "PythonProgram::callFunction()" calls any callable global or builtin
      (ex: a class, a \c functools.partial object, \c len), and
      @ref Python::PythonProgram::callMethod() "PythonProgram::callMethod()" any callable attribute of the class (only
      plain functions were found); %Python iterators (ex: generators) are %Qore iterators; \c __all__ is honored when
      it is a list (only tuples were); a %Qore module that exists but cannot be loaded raises \c ImportError with the
      %Qore error instead of \c ModuleNotFoundError; the static \c PythonProgram::eval*() methods raise
      \c PYTHON-ERROR instead of asserting when there is no Python program context
      (<a href="https://github.com/qoretechnologies/qore/issues/5501">issue 5501</a>)
    - a %Qore object is the same %Python object each time it is passed to %Python (each pass created a new object,
      and objects were not equal); reading a missing member raises \c AttributeError instead of returning \c None
      (so \c hasattr() and \c getattr() with a default work; private members, and objects of %Python subclasses that
      did not call the %Qore constructor, raised \c QoreException or gave \c None); setting attributes sets %Qore
      members, also from %Python subclasses (which set %Python attributes that %Qore did not see); \c dir() lists
      the public members; %Qore objects support weak references (see @ref python_qore_objects;
      <a href="https://github.com/qoretechnologies/qore/issues/5500">issue 5500</a>)
    - the public static members of %Qore classes, %Qore enums and hashdecls are available in %Python (they were not
      imported): static members are attributes giving and setting their current values, enums are %Python enum
      classes whose members are the %Qore enum values, and hashdecls are \c dict subclasses whose objects are hashes
      of the hashdecl in %Qore; hashes of hashdecls are objects of these classes in %Python (they were plain dicts)
      (see @ref python_qore_declarations; <a href="https://github.com/qoretechnologies/qore/issues/5500">issue
      5500</a>)
    - %Python class methods (\c \@classmethod) can be called from %Qore: they are static methods that get the class
      they are called for, also when inherited (calling one raised \c PYTHON-ERROR, and class methods were missing
      from exported classes); callable attributes of %Python objects (ex: a \c lambda stored in the object) can be
      called as methods; methods that are not methods of the %Qore class (ex: added to the %Python class later) get
      their first argument, which was dropped; all the base classes of a %Python class are parent classes of its
      %Qore class (only the first was); assigning, removing and deleting members of the %Qore objects of %Python
      objects, also of %Qore subclasses of %Python classes, sets and deletes the %Python attributes (assignments
      changed only the %Qore object), and the attributes of the %Python objects of %Qore subclasses are members
      (requires %Qore 3.0) (see @ref python_classes_in_qore;
      <a href="https://github.com/qoretechnologies/qore/issues/5499">issue 5499</a>)
    - %Python code calls the %Qore overrides of the methods of a %Python class that a %Qore class inherits (ex:
      \c self.hook() in a method of the %Python class called the %Python method), also from \c __init__(), and the
      %Python object of such a %Qore object is the %Qore object when it is returned to %Qore (see
      @ref python_qore_overrides; <a href="https://github.com/qoretechnologies/qore/issues/5499">issue 5499</a>)
    - keyword arguments in calls from %Python to %Qore callables and constructors raise a \c TypeError, as they do for
      %Qore functions and methods, instead of being ignored (ex: <tt>Mutex(bogus=1)</tt> created a \c Mutex)
      (<a href="https://github.com/qoretechnologies/qore/issues/5498">issue 5498</a>)
    - exceptions are converted consistently and without losing information between %Qore and %Python (see
      @ref python_exceptionsguide): %Qore exceptions are raised in %Python as \c qoreloader.QoreException (a
      \c RuntimeError that can be imported with <tt>from qoreloader import QoreException</tt>, one for each
      interpreter) with \c err, \c desc, \c arg, location and call stack attributes, and their chained exceptions as
      \c __cause__; previously embedded %Python code got a \c RuntimeError without these attributes, and the class
      could not be imported.  A \c QoreException raised in %Python is a %Qore exception with its \c err, \c desc and
      \c arg, while a %Python \c RuntimeError with two or three arguments is no longer taken for one.  The \c arg of
      the %Qore exception for a %Python exception is the exception object (it was always empty), the cause or
      context of a %Python exception is chained to it as \c next, and the internal C++ entry is no longer in the call
      stack.  A %Python exception that passes through %Qore code is raised again in %Python as the original object
      (\c SystemExit raised in a %Python callback of %Qore code now exits the program instead of becoming a
      \c QoreException), and a %Qore exception that passes through %Python code is raised again in %Qore with its
      location, call stack and chained exceptions.  The \c err of an exception of a class defined in C uses the
      class's module and qualified name (ex: \c "decimal.InvalidOperation"), and the %Qore call stack of %Qore code
      called from %Python includes all %Python frames of the call: the innermost frame was missing, and calls of
      %Qore functions imported into %Python had none
      (<a href="https://github.com/qoretechnologies/qore/issues/5497">issue 5497</a>)
    - the destructor of a %Qore class runs for the objects of its %Python subclasses (also through other %Python
      classes); previously it never ran, not even with @ref delete "delete", as the %Qore class was a virtual base
      class of the class created for the %Python subclass
      (<a href="https://github.com/qoretechnologies/qore/issues/5508">issue 5508</a>)
    - threads created by %Python that call %Qore concurrently no longer use the %Python thread state of an ended
      thread, which aborted free-threaded %Python (\c "_PyThreadState_Attach: non-NULL old thread state") and could
      corrupt the thread states of an interpreter: the cleanup of an ended thread was skipped when another thread held
      the module's thread lock, and the next thread given its %Qore thread ID found its thread state; a cached thread
      state is now also only used by the native thread that it belongs to
      (<a href="https://github.com/qoretechnologies/qore/issues/5494">issue 5494</a>)
    - %Qore code that runs when %Python releases %Qore values (the destructors of objects held by a %Qore closure or
      call reference that %Python releases, of the arguments and results of calls from %Python to %Qore, and of
      member values read from %Python) runs without the GIL, so it can no longer deadlock with a thread that holds a
      %Qore lock and waits for the GIL; the GIL is released only when releasing the value can run %Qore code
      (<a href="https://github.com/qoretechnologies/qore/issues/5493">issue 5493</a>)
    - %Python objects for %Qore objects hold strong references, so a %Qore object exists as long as %Qore or %Python
      references it and its destructor runs when the last reference is released; previously every %Qore object passed
      to %Python was added to the thread-local \c "_python_save" list (once for each time it was passed), so it lived
      until the thread ended, and objects created from %Python were saved there as well.  %Qore objects are no longer
      saved in thread-local data; the save object callback is still called for the objects created from %Python.  An
      instance of a %Python subclass of a %Qore class is released by %Python's cycle collector when only it references
      its %Qore object (it was never released).  The destructors of %Qore objects released by %Python, and the save
      object callback, run without the GIL, so they cannot deadlock with a thread that holds a %Qore lock and waits for
      the GIL (<a href="https://github.com/qoretechnologies/qore/issues/5492">issue 5492</a>)
    - deep recursion in %Python code called from a %Qore thread with a small stack raises \c RecursionError instead of
      crashing the process with %Python 3.11 - 3.13: the C recursion counter of the thread state is now reduced to the
      levels that the remaining stack of the %Qore thread can hold; the module previously lowered the recursion limit
      of the thread state, which %Python does not use to stop the recursion
      (<a href="https://github.com/qoretechnologies/qore/issues/5496">issue 5496</a>)
    - \c %%requires \c python no longer hides %Qore's @ref Qore::exit() "exit()": the constants of %Python's
      \c builtins module, including its \c exit and \c quit objects, are no longer imported into the
      \c Python::builtins namespace (reading such a constant also aborted the process at exit); its functions and
      classes still are.  %Python methods named \c constructor, \c destructor, \c methodGate, \c memberGate or
      \c memberNotification are imported with a \c _ prefix, as \c copy already was, instead of tripping an
      assertion or being uncallable (<a href="https://github.com/qoretechnologies/qore/issues/5495">issue 5495</a>)
    - subclasses of the %Python types converted to %Qore (ex: \c enum.IntEnum, \c collections.OrderedDict,
      \c collections.defaultdict) are converted as their base types instead of being wrapped objects; named tuples are
      hashes of their fields, \c set and \c frozenset values are lists and \c memoryview values are binaries; a
      \c dict with keys that give the same %Qore hash key (ex: \c 1 and \c '1') raises an exception instead of losing
      a value (<a href="https://github.com/qoretechnologies/qore/issues/5491">issue 5491</a>)
    - absolute date/time values keep their instant between %Qore and %Python: the UTC offset of an aware
      \c datetime.datetime west of UTC was read without its days (-05:00 became +19:00), UTC was read as the current
      time zone, and %Qore values in other time zones were naive \c datetime.datetime values of their wall-clock time;
      a %Qore value in another time zone is now an aware \c datetime.datetime with its UTC offset.  Relative date/time
      values of more than about 24 days of hours no longer overflow, and \c datetime.timedelta values compare equal
      after a round trip (-1 second came back as -1 day + 86399 seconds).  A \c datetime.time is a %Qore date on
      1970-01-01 instead of a date in year 0, which could not be converted back
      (<a href="https://github.com/qoretechnologies/qore/issues/5490">issue 5490</a>)
    - %Qore \c number values were converted to \c None in %Python, so %Python \c int values outside the 64-bit range
      did not survive a round trip: a number is now a %Python \c int (an exact integral value outside the 64-bit
      range) or a \c decimal.Decimal, and a \c decimal.Decimal is a %Qore \c number.  A %Qore value of a type that
      cannot be converted to %Python raises a \c PYTHON-VALUE-ERROR exception instead of becoming \c None, and a
      namespace constant that cannot be converted is skipped when a namespace is imported into %Python, as class
      constants are (<a href="https://github.com/qoretechnologies/qore/issues/5489">issue 5489</a>)
    - %Python values that a %Qore value cannot represent raise an exception instead of crashing the process: a
      \c list or \c tuple that contains itself, a string or \c dict key with lone surrogates, and an \c int with more
      than 4300 digits (or more significant bits than a %Qore \c number can hold); a \c dict referenced more than
      once in a value is converted each time, where it was dropped or tripped an assertion, a \c dict that contains
      itself raises an exception instead of losing the key, and negative ints between -10^18 and the minimum 64-bit
      integer convert to \c int instead of \c number
      (<a href="https://github.com/qoretechnologies/qore/issues/5488">issue 5488</a>)
    - fixed crashes when a thread created by %Python (ex: \c threading.Thread) called a %Qore function or callable,
      constructed a %Qore object, read a member of a %Qore object, imported a %Qore class or released the last
      reference to a %Qore callable; such a thread is now registered with %Qore while it runs, so the %Qore objects
      it creates are kept and the %Qore resources it acquires (ex: a lock) stay its own from one call to the next, as
      in a %Qore thread, and it is deregistered when it ends
    - %Qore code called from a thread created by %Python (ex: a %Qore closure or a class from a %Qore module) runs
      under the @ref sandboxing "sandbox" of the %Program whose %Python code runs on the thread, as when that code is
      called on a %Qore thread; previously it ran with no sandbox
    - changes made to \c os.environ (and with \c os.putenv() and \c os.unsetenv()) are serialized with the
      environment functions of %Qore, which read and change the environment under one lock; previously %Python changed
      the environment while %Qore threads could read it, which could crash the process.  The environment variables that
      the module reads (\c PYTHONHOME, \c PYTHONPATH) and the environment that %Python copies into \c os.environ when
      it starts are read under the lock as well.
    - the copy of the environment that each %Python interpreter makes into \c os.environ when it is created, and the
      \c TZ variable that its \c time module reads when it is imported, are read under the environment lock of
      %Qore; previously a %Python interpreter created while a %Qore thread changed the
      environment could read it while it changed
    - a child process created with @ref Qore::fork() "fork()" while another thread holds a lock of the module can
      use %Python, as long as no other thread held the %Python GIL; previously the child could wait forever for a lock
      held by a thread that did not exist in it
    - \c os.fork() (and \c os.forkpty()) called in a %Python interpreter of the module (every interpreter of a
      @ref Python::PythonProgram "PythonProgram" or of a @ref Qore::Program "Program" using %Python, which are
      %Python subinterpreters) now raises a %Python \c RuntimeError; previously the child process died with "Fatal
      Python error: _PyInterpreterState_DeleteExceptMain: not main interpreter", as %Python supports \c fork() only in
      its main interpreter.  Processes can still be started (ex: with \c subprocess).
    - a child process created with @ref Qore::fork() "fork()" while other threads were using a %Python interpreter
      (ex: a %Qore thread in a call from %Python to %Qore, or a thread created by %Python) can delete the interpreter;
      previously the deletion waited forever for those threads, which do not exist in the child.  Such an interpreter
      is not finalized in the child if threads created by %Python in the parent had a state in it; it is left in
      memory.  New threads of the child that use an interpreter get their own %Python thread states.
    - @ref Qore::fork() "fork()" called from %Python code in a %Python process using %Qore through the \c qoreloader
      module runs %Python's fork protocol, as \c os.fork() does: the functions registered with
      \c os.register_at_fork() run, and the child no longer keeps the %Python state of the threads of the parent;
      previously the child's exit waited forever for the threads of the parent that are not daemon threads.  While
      %Python interpreters of %Qore exist in the process (@ref Python::PythonProgram "PythonProgram" objects, or
      @ref Qore::Program "Program" objects using %Python), the fork is made as before, since %Python's fork protocol
      crashes the child then (<a href="https://github.com/python/cpython/issues/139759">CPython issue 139759</a>,
      which \c os.fork() also has); see @ref python_fork
    - a child process created with @ref Qore::fork() "fork()" while another thread held the %Python GIL, which can
      never be released in the child, raises a \c PYTHON-FORK-ERROR exception when it calls %Python or creates a
      %Python interpreter, and exits normally; previously it waited forever for the GIL.  If the thread that called
      @ref Qore::fork() "fork()" was called from %Python, the child cannot return to %Python code: it writes an error
      message to \c stderr and exits with status 1.  This requires the internal C headers of %Python
      (\c internal/pycore_*.h, installed with the %Python headers) when the module is built.
    - a %Qore callable (closure or call reference) called from %Python runs without the %Python GIL, as %Qore
      functions and methods called from %Python do; previously a callable that waited for another %Python thread
      waited forever
    - fixed incomplete finalization of Python subinterpreters, including atexit hooks, free-threaded builds,
      and destruction on a different native thread
    - with %Python 3.12 and earlier, destroying a %Python interpreter in a thread that has the identifier of the
      thread that imported \c threading in it and has ended (thread identifiers are reused) no longer reports
      "Exception ignored in threading._shutdown" with an \c AssertionError, and now waits for the threads of the
      interpreter that are not daemon threads, which it did not do in this case
    - with %Python 3.13 and earlier, destroying a %Python interpreter (ex: a @ref Python::PythonProgram "PythonProgram"
      object, or a @ref Qore::Program "Program" using %Python) while a %Python daemon thread still runs in it no longer
      aborts the process with "Fatal Python error: Py_EndInterpreter: not the last thread": such a thread cannot be
      waited for, so the interpreter is left in memory with the thread running in it, and a call it makes to %Qore
      afterwards raises a %Python \c RuntimeError; with %Python 3.14, the interpreter is finalized and the thread exits
      when it next runs.  The interpreter still waits for the threads that are not daemon threads, as in %Python.
    - fixed a hang finalizing a Python subinterpreter with Python 3.12 and earlier on a native thread other than the
      thread that imported \c threading in it (ex: a @ref Qore::Program "Program" using %Python destroyed in another
      thread than the one that created it): the finalization waited for that thread's state, which was never deleted
    - fixed interpreter ownership of imported Qore wrappers when independent PythonProgram objects share a Qore program
    - preserve Qore proxies for Python objects passed between interpreters, preventing cross-interpreter garbage
      collection while preserving object identity
    - resolve import specifications and stack-inspection callables in their owning interpreter and propagate
      import-spec allocation failures without corrupting subsequent imports
    - fixed retained return values, constructed Qore objects, and Python references held by wrappers or callbacks after
      their interpreter is deleted
    - fixed heap-type reference counting and garbage collection of Qore wrapper instances
    - fixed retained traceback frames after converting Python exceptions to Qore exceptions
    - release generated wrapper metadata when hosted by native Python; retained Python functions and bound methods
      now own their metadata and report an error if called after Qore shutdown
    - fixed a crash importing %Qore classes into %Python when a class constant has no %Python representation;
      such constants (ex: \c java.time.Instant::MIN, whose year lies outside the range supported by
      \c datetime.datetime) are now skipped instead of terminating the process
    - fixed %Qore date/time values with a year outside the range of a 16-bit integer being silently converted to
      an unrelated date/time value; such values are now reported as errors
    - fixed error reporting when a %Qore value cannot be converted to a %Python value; conversion failures now
      always raise a %Qore exception

    <b>Build System Changes:</b>
    - Requires a C++17-compatible compiler

    @section python_1_2_1 python Module Version 1.2.1
    - fixed a thread-safety race condition in \c qpy_deregister() where the shared program set was accessed without
      proper locking
      (<a href="https://github.com/qorelanguage/qore/issues/5048">issue 5048</a>)
    - fixed an incorrect assertion in \c getQoreDateTimeFromTime() that used \c PyDateTime_Check instead of checking
      for \c TimeType
      (<a href="https://github.com/qorelanguage/qore/issues/5049">issue 5049</a>)
    - removed a debug \c printf() statement that was left in \c callPythonMethod()
      (<a href="https://github.com/qorelanguage/qore/issues/5050">issue 5050</a>)
    - fixed a typo in an error message ("dictiomary" -> "dictionary")
      (<a href="https://github.com/qorelanguage/qore/issues/5051">issue 5051</a>)

    @section python_1_2 python Module Version 1.2
    - enable proper Python stack trace reporting when exceptions are thrown in %Qore code called from %Python; %Python
      stack frames are now included in the %Qore stack trace
      (<a href="https://github.com/qorelanguage/qore/issues/4653">issue 4653</a>)

    @section python_1_1_7 python Module Version 1.1.7
    - enabled building with standard atomic operations for all platforms

    @section python_1_1_6 python Module Version 1.1.6
    - fixed a memory leak with subinterpreters when the module is initialized by python through the
      @ref python_qoreloader_moduleguide "qoreloader" module
      (<a href="https://github.com/qorelanguage/qore/issues/4652">issue 4652</a>)
    - fixed a crash on exit when %Qore and the python module were initialized from Java
      (<a href="https://github.com/qorelanguage/qore/issues/4651">issue 4651</a>)

    @section python_1_1_5 python Module Version 1.1.5
    - updated to support Python 3.11
      (<a href="https://github.com/qorelanguage/qore/issues/4650">issue 4650</a>)
    - fixed a crash related to Python subinterpreter cleanup when the module is loaded by native Python
      (<a href="https://github.com/qorelanguage/qore/issues/4649">issue 4649</a>)

    @section python_1_1_4 python Module Version 1.1.4
    - another fix handling subinterpreters
      (<a href="https://github.com/qorelanguage/qore/issues/4405">issue 4405</a>)

    @section python_1_1_3 python Module Version 1.1.3
    - @ref Python::PythonProgram::evalExpression() "PythonProgram::evalExpression()" not able to reference
      symbols in the program
      (<a href="https://github.com/qorelanguage/qore/issues/4406">issue 4406</a>)
    - subinterpreter errors using the \c python module from %Python
      (<a href="https://github.com/qorelanguage/qore/issues/4405">issue 4405</a>)

    @section python_1_1_2 python Module Version 1.1.2
    - not possible to use the \c qoreloader module from native %Python
      (<a href="https://github.com/qorelanguage/qore/issues/4398">issue 4398</a>)
    - not possible to call imported Java or %Qore static methods when a normal method exists in the same class with
